    <!-- ── Alert Routes ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="card doc-section" id="alert-routes">
      <div class="card-header">
        <span class="card-header-icon">📡</span>
        <div>
          <div class="card-title">Alert Routes</div>
          <div class="card-subtitle">Route specific solutions to dedicated Teams channels</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card-body prose">
        <p>Alert Routes allow different solutions to post to different Teams channels with optional per-route minimum severity. Each route is a record in the <strong>ppmf_alertroute</strong> table, manageable from the <strong>Setup &amp; Configuration</strong> page or the <strong>Configuration → Alert Routes</strong> view.</p>

        <p><strong>How routing works at runtime:</strong> when a flow failure arrives, the error handler queries <code>ppmf_alertroutes</code> filtered to active routes where <code>ppmf_solutionnamepattern</code> is blank (wildcard) or exactly equals the incoming <code>SolutionName</code>. The first matching route's Team ID and Channel ID override the environment variable defaults via <code>coalesce()</code>. If no route matches, the default env var channel is used.</p>

        <p><strong>Fallback order:</strong></p>
        <ol style="margin:8px 0 12px 20px;font-size:13px;color:var(--text-secondary);line-height:1.8">
          <li>Active Alert Route with exact <code>ppmf_solutionnamepattern</code> match</li>
          <li>Active Alert Route with blank <code>ppmf_solutionnamepattern</code> (wildcard)</li>
          <li><code>ppmf_TeamID</code> and <code>ppmf_FlowAlertsChannelID</code> environment variables</li>
        </ol>

        <div class="callout callout-info">
          <span class="callout-icon">ℹ️</span>
          <div>The <code>ppmf_solutionnamepattern</code> match is an <strong>exact equality</strong> check, not a prefix or contains match. The string your flow passes as <code>SolutionName</code> must exactly equal the pattern on the route record, including case.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

